  "content": [
    {
      "id": "introduction",
      "title": "Introduction",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "String DP is just 2D DP applied to strings. Instead of a grid of cells, your two dimensions are&nbsp;<strong>positions in a string or between two strings</strong>.",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "1-the-core-idea",
      "title": "1. The Core Idea",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In grid DP, you moved through rows and columns. In string DP, you move through&nbsp;<strong>characters</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-527677979.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\"><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Same table. Same filling order. Just different meaning behind the indices.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "2-what-the-table-represents",
      "title": "2. What the Table Represents",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Say you have two strings:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "s1 = \"ACE\"\ns2 = \"BD\""
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Your dp table looks like this:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "code",
          "items": [],
          "codes": {
            "javascript": "\"\"    B     D\n  \"\"  [  0,   0,    0  ]\n  A   [  0,   ?,    ?  ]\n  C   [  0,   ?,    ?  ]\n  E   [  0,   ?,    ?  ]"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each cell&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j]</code>&nbsp;answers the question for the&nbsp;<strong>first i characters of s1</strong>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<strong>first j characters of s2</strong>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The empty string row and column are always your base cases comparing anything to an empty string gives a known answer.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "3-the-key-decision-at-every-cell",
      "title": "3. The Key Decision at Every Cell",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At every cell&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j]</code>&nbsp;you look at the current characters of both strings and ask one question:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-84757741.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-84757741.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-84757741.png",
          "alt": "The Key Decision at Every Cell"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At every cell&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j]</code>, you compare the current characters of both strings.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If the characters match, you take the diagonal value&nbsp;<code>dp[i-1][j-1]</code>&nbsp;and extend it, because both characters are used.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "If the characters do not match, you choose the best between&nbsp;<code>dp[i-1][j]</code>&nbsp;(skip from first string) and&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j-1]</code>&nbsp;(skip from second string).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "So each cell is decided by either extending a match or taking the best possible result by skipping one character.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "4-single-string-vs-two-string-problems",
      "title": "4. Single String vs Two String Problems",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "String DP comes in two <b>flavors</b>:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Two String Problems",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You are given two different strings, so your DP table has size&nbsp;<code>(m+1) × (n+1)</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-183790653.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Each cell&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j]</code>&nbsp;compares characters from both strings:&nbsp;<code>s1[i]</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>s2[j]</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "It represents the answer using the first&nbsp;<code>i</code>&nbsp;characters of&nbsp;<code>s1</code>&nbsp;and first&nbsp;<code>j</code>&nbsp;characters of&nbsp;<code>s2</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "At every step, you decide whether to match characters or skip from one of the strings.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Common examples include Longest Common Subsequence, Edit Distance, and Shortest Common Supersequence.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading3",
          "text": "Single String Problems",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "You are given one string, and you compare it with itself, so the DP table is&nbsp;<code>n × n</code>.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<img src=\"/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197492-651609613.png\" alt=\"image.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 16px 0px; border-radius: 4px;\">",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Each cell&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">dp[i][j]</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;represents a result for the substring from index&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">i</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;to&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">j</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">.</span><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "callout",
          "text": "Instead of comparing two strings, you are analyzing different parts of the same string.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "The focus is usually on substrings or subsequences within that string.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Common examples include Longest Palindromic Subsequence and Palindrome Partitioning.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "5-how-filling-direction-changes",
      "title": "5. How Filling Direction Changes",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For two string problems fill&nbsp;<strong>top to bottom, left to right</strong>. Each cell needs the cell above, the cell to the left, and the diagonal.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-680142302.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-680142302.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-680142302.png",
          "alt": "How Filling Direction Changes"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "For single string problems you often fill&nbsp;<strong>diagonally&nbsp;</strong>or by&nbsp;<strong>increasing substring length</strong>, because<code>dp[i][j]</code>depends on<code>dp[i+1][j-1]</code>which is a smaller substring inside.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-313402456.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-313402456.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-313402456.png",
          "alt": "How Filling Direction Changes"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "heading2",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "6-the-recurrence-is-everything",
      "title": "6. The Recurrence is Everything",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "In string DP, once you clearly define what&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j]</code>&nbsp;means, the rest becomes straightforward.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "F<b>irst, define the meaning of</b>&nbsp;<code>dp[i][j]</code>&nbsp;precisely.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Then ask what should happen when the current characters match.</span><br>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">Then ask what should happen when they do not match.</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "These two cases directly form your recurrence relation.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>The base cases are usually simple:</b><br>",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "list",
          "items": [
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">If one string is empty, the answer is either 0 or depends on length.</span><br>",
            "<span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">If you are working on a single string, a single character case like&nbsp;</span><code style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">dp[i][i] = 1</code><span style=\"letter-spacing: -0.003em;\">&nbsp;is often used.</span><br>"
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "divider",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "7-why-strings-are-a-natural-fit-for-dp",
      "title": "7. Why Strings Are a Natural Fit for DP",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Strings have two properties that make DP work perfectly:",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "items": [],
          "url": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-799965644.png",
          "caption": "",
          "content": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-799965644.png",
          "text": "/engineering/dsa-media/1785406197643-799965644.png",
          "alt": "Why Strings Are a Natural Fit for DP"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "Strings naturally create overlapping subproblems because the same smaller comparisons repeat again and again.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>For example</b>, while comparing&nbsp;<code>\"ABC\"</code>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<code>\"AC\"</code>, you end up solving smaller parts like&nbsp;<code>\"AB\"</code>&nbsp;vs&nbsp;<code>\"A\"</code>&nbsp;multiple times.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "They also have optimal substructure, meaning the answer for the full strings is built using answers from smaller substrings.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "<b>At each step</b>, comparing characters either extends a previous result (when they match) or chooses the best among previous results (when they don’t).",
          "items": []
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "text": "That repeated reuse + building from smaller answers is exactly why DP fits perfectly for string problems.",
          "items": []
        },
        {
